# Skill: Run pytest and log results

Use the helper `scripts/run_pytest.sh` to run pytest against a file or directory
and capture the full combined stdout+stderr (including errors and tracebacks) to
a log file. The script appends an `EXIT CODE: N` line and exits with pytest's own
code, so the pass/fail result is unambiguous.

## How to run

```bash
# Run a single test file; log auto-named under logs/
scripts/run_pytest.sh path/to/test_file.py

# Run a directory with an explicit log path
scripts/run_pytest.sh path/to/tests/ -l logs/run.log
```

Key options: `-l/--log <file>` (default `logs/pytest_<target>_<timestamp>.log`),
`-k/--filter <expr>`, `-m/--mark <expr>`, `-x/--exitfirst`, `-v/--verbose`,
`-j/--jobs <N>` (needs pytest-xdist), `--no-tee` (log only, no console echo),
`--dry-run`. Extra raw pytest args go after `--`.

## Agent guidance

- **Always note the log path** reported on stderr (`log: <path>`) and read it to
  diagnose failures rather than re-running blindly.
- The exit code is pytest's: `0` all passed, `1` tests failed, `5` no tests
  collected, `2`/`4` usage/internal errors. Report failures honestly with the
  relevant log excerpt.
- Prefer `-x` while iterating on a single failing test, and `-k <expr>` to narrow
  scope.
- For flaky or slow suites, use `-j <N>` to parallelize when pytest-xdist is
  available.
- Do not edit tests to make them pass; fix the code under test or report the
  real failure.

## Examples

```bash
scripts/run_pytest.sh tests/test_lowering.py -x -v
scripts/run_pytest.sh tests/ -k hivm -l logs/hivm.log
scripts/run_pytest.sh tests/ -- --maxfail=3 --tb=short
```
